Description: nouveau: add a render backend that exports Chrome's buffers from the system dma-heap
 Lets Android render on an NVIDIA dGPU (zink on NVK) while Chrome keeps the Intel
 iGPU that drives the panel, on the dual-GPU openfyde_iris boards. Until now the
 only nouveau "backend" was the dumb allocator, which cannot create buffers on a
 render node, and gralloc always opened the first render node (i915). i915's
 default layouts are Y/X_TILED; imported into NVK they do not fail, they read as
 garbage (platform_android retries the modifier as MOD_INVALID and zink treats
 that as LINEAR on NVK), about 47% of the pixels wrong in a readback probe.
 .
 The backend makes the same split as v3d on Raspberry Pi. Buffers that stay in
 Android are nouveau GEM objects (VRAM); an importer on the same GPU gets the
 same object back from PRIME. Buffers another device reads -- TaskRenderSurface's
 BO_USE_ARC_COMPOSER_EXPORT buffers, video and camera -- come from
 /dev/dma_heap/system and are PRIME-imported into nouveau, so Chrome's i915
 imports plain system memory. The heap is opened lazily, since only the
 allocator ever allocates. CPU mapping goes through a dma-buf fd, because TTM
 refuses to fault in pages of an imported object (TTM_TT_FLAG_EXTERNAL).
 .
 Row strides are padded to a multiple of 32 bytes. NVK imports a LINEAR buffer
 with exactly the stride gralloc reports, and its texture header can only
 express 32-byte pitches; anything else trips an assertion in nil
 (descriptor.rs, nvb097_fill_image_view_desc) under an extern "C" boundary and
 aborts the process. org.chromium.arc.home did exactly that on its first frame
 at 1366 px (5464 B), which 1920/3200 px tests never show.
 .
 cros_gralloc now honours drm.gpu.vendor_name, the property Mesa's Android EGL
 uses to pick its GPU (droid_open_device), so gralloc and EGL always agree.
 Unset, nothing changes. arc-setup emits it when an NVK-capable NVIDIA GPU is
 present (project-fydroid arc-setup 010).
 .
 Verified on device (i915 ADL-P + RTX 2050): SF on "zink Vulkan 1.4(NVIDIA GeForce
 RTX 2050 (NVK GA107))", boot_completed, Settings on the panel checked by the
 user; its TaskRenderSurface buffers are system-heap dma-bufs with a nouveau
 write fence and i915 read fences, 0 Chrome PRIME errors; readback probe PASS at
 1366x769/1365x767/3200x2000 for app, texture, export and linear usages. Intel
 regression on device (UHD 620 only): SF on iris, probe matrix byte-identical to
 the previous gralloc.
diff --git a/cros_gralloc/cros_gralloc_driver.cc b/cros_gralloc/cros_gralloc_driver.cc
index 799d395..49c11ad 100644
--- a/cros_gralloc/cros_gralloc_driver.cc
+++ b/cros_gralloc/cros_gralloc_driver.cc
@@ -114,6 +114,47 @@ static struct driver *init_try_node(int idx, char const *str)
 	return drv;
 }
 
+/*
+ * On a machine with more than one GPU, Mesa's Android EGL renders on the DRM
+ * driver named by drm.gpu.vendor_name (droid_open_device in
+ * platform_android.c) and otherwise on the first render node that works.
+ * Buffers must come from that same GPU's backend, or the renderer is handed
+ * layouts only the other GPU can read (i915's tiled buffers read as garbage on
+ * nouveau, with no error). Follow the same property, so gralloc and EGL always
+ * pick the same GPU. When the property is unset nothing changes.
+ */
+static struct driver *init_try_vendor_render_node()
+{
+	char vendor[PROPERTY_VALUE_MAX];
+	char node[64];
+
+	if (property_get("drm.gpu.vendor_name", vendor, "") <= 0)
+		return nullptr;
+
+	for (uint32_t i = DRM_RENDER_NODE_START; i < DRM_RENDER_NODE_START + DRM_NUM_NODES; i++) {
+		snprintf(node, sizeof(node), "%s/renderD%u", DRM_DIR_NAME, i);
+		int fd = open(node, O_RDWR, 0);
+		if (fd < 0)
+			continue;
+
+		drmVersionPtr version = drmGetVersion(fd);
+		bool match = version && !strcmp(version->name, vendor);
+		drmFreeVersion(version);
+		if (match) {
+			struct driver *drv = drv_create(fd, NULL);
+			if (drv) {
+				ALOGI("Using %s (drm.gpu.vendor_name) for gralloc.", node);
+				return drv;
+			}
+		}
+		close(fd);
+	}
+
+	ALOGE("drm.gpu.vendor_name=%s matches no usable render node; using the first GPU.",
+	      vendor);
+	return nullptr;
+}
+
 static struct driver *init_try_nodes()
 {
 	/*
@@ -123,7 +164,10 @@ static struct driver *init_try_nodes()
 	 * TODO(gsingh): Enable render nodes on udl/evdi.
 	 */
 
-	struct driver *drv;
+	struct driver *drv = init_try_vendor_render_node();
+	if (drv)
+		return drv;
+
 	char const *render_nodes_fmt = "%s/renderD%d";
 	char const *card_nodes_fmt = "%s/card%d";
 	uint32_t num_nodes = DRM_NUM_NODES;
